##
    Asking a question of something that is not an answer.

    This program does not compile. Six places take a condition, and every one of them wants a
    boolean — not a number that happens to be non-zero, and not an optional that may hold one.

    They are gathered here because they are one rule written six ways. A language that checked
    the 'if' and let the 'while' through would be a language where the same mistake is caught
    or not depending on which line it landed on.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.AnIf();
        Program.AWhile();
        Program.AnUntil();
        Program.AnIfExpression();
        Program.AnOperand();
        Program.ANegation();
        Program.AnOptionalHoldingOne();
    end function

    # A statement's condition, which is the one most people meet first.
    function AnIf()
        integer count = 3;

        if count
            Console.WriteLine("counted");
        end if
    end function

    # The same question, asked by a loop that tests before each turn.
    function AWhile()
        integer count = 3;

        loop while count
            Console.WriteLine("counting");
        end loop
    end function

    # And by the loop that tests after one, which is closed by the condition rather than by 'end'.
    function AnUntil()
        integer count = 3;

        loop
            Console.WriteLine("counting");
        until count
    end function

    ##
        The expression form. It reads differently from the statement and is checked the same,
        which is the point of writing it down beside the others.
    ##
    function AnIfExpression()
        integer count = 3;
        string said = if count then "some" else "none";

        Console.WriteLine(said);
    end function

    # An operand of 'and' or 'or', where the condition is not the whole expression.
    function AnOperand()
        integer count = 3;

        Console.WriteLine(count and true);
    end function

    ##
        'not' answers differently (PC0304), because the question is not "is this a condition"
        but "is this operator defined for this". The mistake is the same one and the reader is
        told the same thing about it.
    ##
    function ANegation()
        integer count = 3;

        Console.WriteLine(not count);
    end function

    ##
        An optional is refused for a reason of its own: it may hold the answer rather than be
        one. 'Or' says what to use when it holds nothing, and a check proves it holds something
        — either reaches a boolean the condition can take.
    ##
    function AnOptionalHoldingOne()
        boolean? ready;

        if ready
            Console.WriteLine("ready");
        end if
    end function

end model
